<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · South America</p><h1 class="st-title">First Republic of Venezuela</h1><p class="st-meta">1811 CE – 1812 CE · capital Caracas</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The first Spanish American colony to declare full independence, a federal republic that collapsed within a year amid earthquake and civil war.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> junta of 19 April 1810 removed the captain-general of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/captaincy-general-of-venezuela">Captaincy General of Venezuela</a>, a congress met in 1811. Urged on by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-miranda">Francisco de Miranda</a> and the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Simón Bolívar</a> of the Patriotic Society, it declared independence on 5 July 1811, the first such declaration in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-america">Spanish America</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A federal constitution was adopted in December 1811, with an executive triumvirate including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cristobal-mendoza">Cristóbal Mendoza</a>. The republic abolished titles and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>, but kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> and property qualifications for voting. Provinces such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coro-venezuela">Coro</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maracaibo">Maracaibo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guayana-province">Guayana</a> stayed royalist. On 26 March 1812 an earthquake destroyed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a> and other patriot towns, and clergy presented it as divine punishment.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The royalist commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/domingo-de-monteverde">Domingo de Monteverde</a> advanced on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caracas">Caracas</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francisco-de-miranda">Miranda</a>, named dictator, capitulated at San Mateo in July 1812. He was handed to the Spanish by fellow officers, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/simon-bolivar">Bolívar</a> among them, and died in a Cádiz prison in 1816.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/first-republic-of-venezuela.jpg" alt="First Republic of Venezuela" width="320" height="213"><figcaption>Image: Salvadoroff · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Segunda_bandera_de_la_Primera_Rep%C3%BAblica_de_Venezuela.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1811 CE – 1812 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Caracas</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Cristóbal Mendoza, Francisco de Miranda</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Roman Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Spanish</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/venezuela">Venezuela</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>American Confederation of Venezuela, Primera República de Venezuela</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1812&amp;focus=first-republic-of-venezuela" aria-label="First Republic of Venezuela on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2818 1801 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where First Republic of Venezuela lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3138 2057L3156 2042M3138 2057L4910 989M3138 2057L3156 2042"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3156 2042h0M4910 989h0M3156 2042h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3138 2057h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3138 2057h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1812&amp;focus=first-republic-of-venezuela">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
